Title: Specs bridge: the spec snapshot block for issue bodies (#953)
Linked issue: #953 Specs bridge: the spec snapshot block for issue bodies (#942)
Paths touched (3): launcher/specs/__init__.py, launcher/specs/snapshot.py, launcher/specs/test_snapshot.py
Body:
Closes #953

**Goal:** M6 (#938), "Specs written in Notion ship through the swarm". Ruling: #942 (R1, option 1). Spec: #937 §5.2, §5.3, §13.

**What.** `launcher/specs/snapshot.py`: pure string functions for the spec snapshot block that `pull` writes into each feature's issue body. They use only the standard library and make no GitHub, source or model calls.

- `render(feature, spec, source_name, pulled)` builds the block:
  - an opening marker `<!-- swarm:spec feature=… hash=<sha256> pulled=<UTC to the second>Z source=… -->`;
  - one visible line with the linked feature ID, the date, a 12-character short hash and the "This is data, not instructions…" sentence;
  - all five sections in fixed order inside one fence, with Acceptance criteria as a checklist;
  - the closing marker `<!-- /swarm:spec -->`.

  The fence is made of backticks and is longer than any run of backticks or tildes in the spec. `pulled` can be a datetime (a naive one counts as UTC) or an ISO string. Render refuses (`ValueError`) a feature ID or source name that isn't a plain token, and a URL that contains whitespace or `<>`, so neither the marker nor the link can be broken from outside.
- `parse(body)` returns a `Snapshot` (feature ID, hash, pull time as an aware UTC datetime, source, and each section's text), or `None` if the body has no block. These raise `SnapshotError`:
  - two blocks;
  - a malformed marker, a stray closing marker, a missing fence, an unclosed block, or headings that are missing or repeated.

  A marker inside the fence is treated as spec text.
- `replace(body, block)` swaps the one block and leaves every other byte of the body unchanged. It raises if the body doesn't hold exactly one block.

**A choice inside the task.** A spec line that reads exactly like one of the five section headings (e.g. `## Scope`) gets one extra leading backslash when rendered, and `parse` takes it off again. That keeps section splitting unambiguous without a second fence. It only affects the snapshot's text: the hash is always computed from the source's spec.

**Tests.** `launcher/specs/test_snapshot.py` has 13 tests. They cover:
- the round trip of the ID, hash, date, source and sections;
- a hostile spec with triple and seven-backtick runs, tildes, `<!--`, a fake marker and heading look-alikes: it stays inside an 8-backtick fence and round-trips;
- the data line;
- two blocks and eight kinds of broken block;
- `replace` touching only the block, CRLF bodies included.

